Ladybird decodes a JPEG with several scans as a buffered image, reads
all the input there is, and outputs its rows from the scans read. When
the input was cut off, it outputs from the scan before the one read
last, and turns off block smoothing. Blink’s JPEGImageReader::Decode()
also decodes such files as buffered images, and starts output with the
scan before the last one unless it reached EOI, but it keeps smoothing
on. And WebKit’s JPEGImageReader::decode() and Gecko’s nsJPEGDecoder::
ReadJPEGData() decode progressive files as buffered images as well.

A 64x48 progressive JPEG cut at 90%, 60% and 30% of its bytes decodes
to the whole image in Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit
26.5, and now in Ladybird. Cut within the header of their second scan,
three progressive JPEGs (64x48, 16x16 and 64x800) decode in full from
their first scan in WebKit and now in Ladybird, with the same colors.
Firefox leaves their last row of blocks out, and Chrome shows nothing,
since block smoothing waits on the next scan. Cut where the first scan
ends, before any marker, WebKit still decodes every row, while Firefox
and Ladybird leave out the last row of blocks, whose final Huffman
codes the decoder only reads once it sees what follows them.

A baseline JPEG cut short decodes differently in each engine: Chrome
draws nothing, Firefox repeats the last rows it read, and WebKit fills
the rest with gray. So Ladybird keeps its own behavior there, which
leaves the rest transparent.
